=== history-blob {#b-7b3ca79d1ba0 lang=geml}
- Style note (non-normative): keep the document title in `=== meta`
  (`title = "…"`), not in a top-level heading — every heading then denotes a
  genuine section of the document.
- **Reading an attribute object.** Every processor MUST read an object the same
  way, because what it yields is the block's model. §3.1's grammar states the
  shapes; these three steps decide every input:
  1. *Where it is.* On a fence line the object opens at the `{` after the type
     name and runs to the **last** `}` of the logical line, which only whitespace
     may follow; a brace between them is an ordinary character. On a heading
     line it is the trailing group: the heading's text, trailing spaces and tabs
     removed, ends in `}`, and reading leftwards from that `}` — a `"` toggles a
     quoted span unless an odd run of `\` precedes it — the nearest `{` outside a
     span, a code span and inline math (§5.3) opens the object when whitespace,
     or the start of the heading's text, precedes it. A `}` met outside a span
     before that `{`, or a `{` with no whitespace before it, leaves the heading
     without an object: `## Title{#a}` and `## a {b}x}` keep all their text.
     After a link or an image (§5.1) the object opens at the `{` directly after
     it and closes at the first `}` outside a quoted span; with no such `}`
     there is none, and the `{` is text.
  2. *Items.* Inside the braces White_Space separates items, except inside a
     quoted span: a `"` anywhere in an item opens one, and the next `"` that `\`
     does not escape closes it. Inside a span `\"` and `\\` are escapes and
     nothing else is; a span left open runs to the end of the object. An item
     that begins with `#` is the id and one that begins with `.` a class; an
     item with `=` after its first character is `key=value`, split at its first
     `=`; any other item is a flag. Of two ids, or two items naming one key, the
     first is the one read.
  3. *Values.* A value that begins and ends with `"` is a quoted string: its
     text is what lies between, `\"` read as `"` and `\\` as `\`. Any other
     value is a bare word, taken verbatim with any quotes it holds, so
     `src=#t[1]["Net revenue"]` needs no outer quotes; a value needs them only to
     hold whitespace outside a span. An id, class or key that is not a NAME is
     still read, and is a `name-not-a-name` warning.
- Attribute value typing: a quoted `"…"` is always a string; `true`/`false` is a
  boolean; a bare word matching integer/float syntax is a number; any other bare
  word is a string. Arrays, dates and nested tables are not supported.
- A bare attribute word with no `=` is a boolean flag set to `true` (e.g.
  `hidden`).
- A `=== meta` block holds document metadata as one `key=val` per line: the key
  is the text before the first `=` and the value everything after it, each
  trimmed of surrounding whitespace, and the value is typed as above. A value
  that is neither quoted, a boolean nor a number is a string however it is
  spelled — `title = hello world` is the string `hello world`, with no quotes
  needed, because a `meta` line is not an attribute object and nothing in it
  ends at whitespace. If a document contains multiple `=== meta` blocks,
  their keys are merged; the **first** definition of a key takes precedence —
  a later definition of the same key is a `duplicate-meta-key` **warning** and
  is ignored. Which blocks count is the ordinary nesting rule of §3: a `=== meta`
  at the top level or inside a `flow` body is a block and its keys are the
  document's, while one appearing inside a `raw` or `data` body is that body's
  content and defines nothing — so a document may show `=== meta` as an example
  inside a longer-fenced `code` block without those keys becoming real.
  `profile` is a **reserved** meta key: it declares the application-layer
  vocabularies this document uses (§8.6), and a processor MUST NOT read it as
  anything else.
  In flow text, `{{key}}` is replaced with the matching
  `meta` value; an unknown key is a build **error**. Interpolation reads the
  flow source text and honors the verbatim atoms of §5.3 phase 1(1): a
  `{{key}}` inside a code span or inline math is left untouched (so a GEML
  document can quote this very syntax), raw block bodies are never
  interpolated, and a backslash-escaped `\{{key}}` renders as the literal text
  `{{key}}`.

===

